The CSI-2 receiver clock gate that mainline does not touch

WHAT IS MISSING
---------------
cam_ife_csid_global_reset(), from the surya's own Android kernel, writes two
things right BEFORE pulsing the CSID reset strobe:

    CSI2_RX_CFG1 = 0x80      bit 7 = CGC_MODE
    RDI_CFG0(i)  = 0x02      for each RDI

Mainline writes neither of the two: it goes straight to the 0x1e strobe.

CGC_MODE is the CSI-2 receiver's clock gating control. A block with its clock
gated off behaves EXACTLY like ours: its registers answer -the register bus is
separate, which is why the CSIPHY's 110 registers read back without a single
failure- and the data path never processes anything.

WHY IT FITS
-----------
Because of the round-9 bisection, which is the strongest datum there is:

    front    Samsung S5K3T2, CSIPHY1, 4 lanes, 19.2 MHz, 845 MHz  -> rx=0
    rear     Sony IMX682,    CSIPHY0, 2 lanes, 24 MHz,  1020 MHz  -> rx=0

Two sensors from two manufacturers, two drivers, two PHYs, two clocks and two
speeds, and neither hands the CSID a single symbol. If it were the sensor's or
the PHY's fault the two would not fail the same way; what they share is the
CSID's receiver.

And the CSID's own pattern generator DOES mark SOT and EOT on all four lanes
(rx = 0x000000ff), that is, the receiver knows how to report when traffic is
injected into it from the inside. What does not work is its outside input.

WHAT IT DOES
------------
It adds the vendor's two writes before the reset strobe, and leaves a second
parameter to also try keeping CGC_MODE set during the transmission.

    cgc_en_reset    default 1, as the vendor does
    cgc_en_marcha   default 0, for sweeping
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid-gen2.c	2026-08-17 12:10:06.918495661 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid-gen2.c	2026-08-17 12:10:28.842650797 +0200
@@ -49,6 +49,16 @@
  * interrupts per frame this can turn into a storm, and this
  * phone is sensitive to timing. Enable it by hand when needed.
  */
+static int cgc_en_reset = 1;
+module_param(cgc_en_reset, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(cgc_en_reset,
+	"Write CGC_MODE and RDI_CFG0=2 before the reset strobe, like the vendor.");
+
+static int cgc_en_marcha;
+module_param(cgc_en_marcha, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(cgc_en_marcha,
+	"Also, leave CGC_MODE set in CSI2_RX_CFG1 during the transmission.");
+
 static int diag_phy_sel = -1;
 module_param(diag_phy_sel, int, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(diag_phy_sel,
@@ -239,6 +249,8 @@
 	if (vc > 3)
 		val |= 1 << CSI2_RX_CFG1_VC_MODE;
 	val |= 1 << CSI2_RX_CFG1_MISR_EN;
+	if (cgc_en_marcha)
+		val |= 1 << CSI2_RX_CFG1_CGC_MODE;
 	writel_relaxed(val, csid->base + CSID_CSI2_RX_CFG1);
 
 	/* DIAGNOSTIC: unmask the receiver if we are asked to. */
@@ -493,6 +505,7 @@
 	printk(KERN_INFO "csid_reset called\n");
 	unsigned long time;
 	u32 val;
+	int i;
 
 	reinit_completion(&csid->reset_complete);
 
@@ -501,6 +514,31 @@
 	writel_relaxed(1, csid->base + CSID_TOP_IRQ_MASK);
 	writel_relaxed(1, csid->base + CSID_IRQ_CMD);
 
+	/*
+	 * WHAT THE VENDOR DOES BEFORE THE STROBE AND MAINLINE DOES NOT.
+	 *
+	 * cam_ife_csid_global_reset(), from the Android kernel of the surya itself,
+	 * writes two things right before pulsing the reset:
+	 *
+	 *     CSI2_RX_CFG1 = 0x80          bit 7 = CGC_MODE
+	 *     RDI_CFG0(i)  = 0x02          for each RDI
+	 *
+	 * CGC_MODE is the clock gating control of the CSI-2 receiver. A
+	 * block with the clock gated behaves EXACTLY like ours:
+	 * its registers answer -the register bus goes separately- and the
+	 * datos no procesa nada nunca.
+	 *
+	 * And it fits the bisection: two different sensors, on two different
+	 * CSIPHYs, with two different drivers, none delivers a single
+	 * symbol to the CSID. If it were the sensor or the PHY it would not be both.
+	 */
+	if (cgc_en_reset) {
+		writel_relaxed(1 << CSI2_RX_CFG1_CGC_MODE,
+			       csid->base + CSID_CSI2_RX_CFG1);
+		for (i = 0; i < MSM_CSID_MAX_SRC_STREAMS; i++)
+			writel_relaxed(0x02, csid->base + CSID_RDI_CFG0(i));
+	}
+
 	/* preserve registers */
 	val = 0x1e << RST_STROBES;
 	writel_relaxed(val, csid->base + CSID_RST_STROBES);
